@using System.Globalization
@using EliteIndustries.Models
@*
    Homepage stats band.

    Four figures on the brand red, divided by hairlines — a spec plate, not
    four cards. It follows the about preview deliberately: that band says who
    the company is and carries no numbers at all, so this one can be nothing
    but numbers and the two do not compete. The ground is red because the bands
    either side are both --ei-shell and one of the three has to break the page;
    the reasoning and every contrast figure live in wwwroot/css/stats.css.

    WHERE THE FIGURES COME FROM
    ---------------------------
    All four read from Models/CompanyProfile.cs and nothing is hard-coded here.
    Three are the client's own, from the counters on eliteind.in; the years is
    computed from FoundedYear. That file is the one place to change any of
    them and this band follows automatically.

    The order, the labels and the "years" set against the first figure all
    match the client's band. The one thing not copied from it is the number
    inside that first figure: theirs reads 14, which was true when the page was
    written and is not now. YearsOfExperience derives it instead, so this one
    cannot go stale the same way.

    THE COUNT-UP
    ------------
    reveal.js already owns both behaviours this band needs and fires each of
    them exactly once, unobserving the element as it lands. So there is no
    script here and no observer of any kind:

      .ei-reveal        the entrance
      [data-count-to]   the count

    Two rules that come with borrowing that counter:

      1. data-count-to must be the bare integer. reveal.js runs it through
         parseFloat, and parseFloat("2,400") is 2.
      2. The element's own text must already be the FINAL figure, grouped the
         same way reveal.js will group it — it formats with toLocaleString on
         en-IN, so the server formats with en-IN too. reveal.js overwrites the
         text only while the animation runs, which means a crawler and a
         visitor with no JS both read the true number rather than a zero.

    There is no suffix support in reveal.js: it assigns to textContent, so
    anything inside the counting element is wiped on the first frame. The
    suffix — "+" on three of the four, "years" on the first — is therefore a
    sibling, outside it.
*@
@{
    // en-IN, to match the locale reveal.js formats with. If those two ever
    // disagree the figure visibly re-groups itself on the last frame of the
    // count, which reads as a glitch.
    var inr = CultureInfo.GetCultureInfo("en-IN");

    // Icons are picked from the trade rather than from a generic set, and each
    // one has to already exist in _Icons.cshtml:
    //   award     — the years, the only earned figure of the four
    //   container — a consignment packed and shipped
    //   users     — the people on the floor
    //   globe     — clients, who are exporters selling into other markets
    //
    // Order and labels are the client's own, in the client's own sequence.
    // The suffix is the client's too: the three counted figures carry "+" and
    // claim only "at least", and the years carries the word instead, because
    // "16+ Experience" says nothing and "16 years" says the whole thing.
    var stats = new[]
    {
        ("i-award",     CompanyProfile.YearsOfExperience, "Experience",        "years"),
        ("i-container", CompanyProfile.ProjectsCompleted, "Complete Projects", "+"),
        ("i-users",     CompanyProfile.Employees,         "Works Employed",    "+"),
        ("i-globe",     CompanyProfile.HappyClients,      "Happy Clients",     "+")
    };
}

<section class="ei-band ei-band--tight ei-stats" aria-labelledby="ei-stats-title">

    @* The brief asks for four stat blocks and no heading, but a landmark with
       no accessible name is just "region" in a screen reader's list. This
       names it without putting a heading on screen that the design does not
       want. *@
    <h2 class="ei-sr" id="ei-stats-title">@CompanyProfile.Name in numbers</h2>

    <div class="ei-shell">
        <div class="ei-stats-row">
            @{ var step = 0; }
            @foreach (var (icon, value, label, suffix) in stats)
            {
                @* 90ms apart: the four cells arrive left to right as one
                   movement rather than together as a block. *@
                <div class="ei-stats-cell ei-reveal" style="--d:@(step * 90)ms">

                    <svg class="ei-stats-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="#@icon" /></svg>

                    <p class="ei-stats-value">
                        @* data-count-to is the bare integer; the text is the
                           grouped final figure. See the note at the top. *@
                        <span class="ei-stats-figure" data-count-to="@value">@value.ToString("N0", inr)</span><span class="ei-stats-suffix @(suffix == "+" ? "" : "ei-stats-suffix--word")">@suffix</span>
                    </p>

                    <p class="ei-stats-label">@label</p>
                </div>

                step++;
            }
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
